[{:registry/operating-states [:intake :propose :approve :execute :audit], :actor/wave 3, :registry/required-technologies [:robotics :identity :audit-ledger], :actor/scope-excluded-ops [:finalize-assembly-decision :finalize-assembly-operation :authorize-assembly-run :proceed-with-assembly-run :finalize-line-safety-clearance :declare-line-safety-cleared :declare-assembly-line-safety-cleared :declare-line-cleared-for-operation :clear-line-for-operation :override-plant-safety-officer-judgment :override-safety-officer-judgment], :actor/robotics? true, :adr/related ["adr-2607121000-cloud-itonami-global-isic-isco-reverse-toposort-plan" "adr-2799008122-cloud-itonami-isco-8122-metal-finishing-plating-coating-machine-operators-coverage"], :adr/status :accepted, :actor/scope-excluded-terms ["proceed with the assembly run" "proceed with the assembly operation" "authorize the assembly run" "authorize the assembly operation" "finalize the assembly decision" "finalize the assembly operation" "declare the line safety cleared" "declare the assembly line safety cleared" "declare the line cleared for operation" "clear the line for operation" "finalize the line safety clearance" "finalize the line-safety clearance" "override the plant safety officer's judgment" "override the safety officer's judgment" "override plant safety officer judgment"], :adr/id 2799008211, :phase/rollout "{0 {:label \"read-only\", :writes #{}, :auto #{}}, 1 {:label \"assisted-logging\", :writes #{:log-work-record}, :auto #{}}, 2 {:label \"assisted-coordination\", :writes #{:log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order}, :auto #{}}, 3 {:label \"supervised-auto\", :writes #{:log-work-record :flag-safety-concern :schedule-crew-operation :coordinate-supply-order}, :auto #{:log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation}}}", :actor/domain :manufacturing/mechanical-machinery-assembly-line-scheduling-logistics-coordination, :actor/proposal-ops [:log-work-record :schedule-crew-operation :flag-safety-concern :coordinate-supply-order], :actor/hard-checks [:no-assembler :no-line :no-actuation :unknown-op :scope-excluded-action], :registry/maturity :implemented, :actor/isco-08 "8211", :actor/always-escalate-ops [:flag-safety-concern], :adr/doc_type "adr", :operation/nodes [:intake :advise :govern :decide :request-approval :commit :hold], :operation/entry-point :intake, :adr/body "# ADR 2799008211: cloud-itonami ISCO-08 8211 Mechanical Machinery Assemblers assembly-line scheduling/logistics coordination coverage\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted\n\n## Context\n\nThis is part of the occupation-classification track (sibling to the\n`cloud-itonami-isic-*` industry-actor rollout, same governing\nADR-2607121000): codify every ISCO-08 occupation unit group as an\nautonomous \"actor\" (LLM/advisor behind an independent Governor, a\n`langgraph.graph` StateGraph, append-only audit ledger), modeled on a\nrobotics premise -- a robot performs the occupation's physical/\nadministrative work, proposing actions to an independent Governor that\ngates them, never dispatches hardware itself, always escalates\nhigh-stakes decisions to a human. This ADR covers **ISCO-08 class 8211\n-- Mechanical Machinery Assemblers** only. This is Wave3 (production/\ntrades); Wave0/1/2 are 100% complete and Wave3 batches 1-14 already\nlanded 84 occupations, leaving 17 gaps; this actor begins the 82xx\nassemblers subgroup (`kotoba.occupation.wave/wave-of \"8211\"` resolves\nto wave 3).\n\n`gh api repos/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8211` returned a clean\n404 before any work began -- this was a genuinely fresh scaffold, not a\nresume of prior partial work.\n\nThe `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's live `{:id \"8211\" ...}` entry\nwas verified against a fresh clone before any work began (`clojure -M\n-e \"(require 'kotoba.occupation) (println (kotoba.occupation/get-\noccupation \\\"8211\\\"))\"` from a checkout with the `kotoba-lang/\ntechnology` sibling present at the correct `../technology` relative\npath), and its `:name` read exactly \"Mechanical Machinery Assemblers\"\n-- confirming the premise, no mismatch. Prior to this ADR the entry's\n`:maturity` was `:spec` with `:required-technologies [:robotics\n:identity :audit-ledger]` and no `:repo`/`:business-id`. This ADR and\nits companion registry edit promote the `8211` entry to\n`:implemented`.\n\n**Assembly-line/workshop mechanical hazard dimension.** Mechanical\nMachinery Assemblers assemble mechanical components on production\nlines -- a standard workshop hazard domain: hand-tool injury and\npinch-point/crush hazard from assembly fixtures and machinery. This\nactor does NOT model direct assembly-execution authority or\nline-safety-clearance authority. It is deliberately restricted to LINE\nSCHEDULING and LOGISTICS COORDINATION around the occupation\n(production-run/inventory/progress-record logging, crew/shift\nscheduling, safety-concern escalation, and components/fasteners-stock\nprocurement coordination) -- never the assembly-execution decision or\nthe line-safety-clearance decision itself, and never an override of a\nplant safety officer's judgment. The closed proposal-op allowlist\nstructurally excludes any op that could directly finalize such a\ndecision, and the governor independently, permanently hard-blocks any\nproposal whose rationale text names such a finalization action,\nregardless of the declared `:op` or confidence -- this is not an\nescalation path a human can approve through, it is an outright,\nstructural refusal at every maturity level.\n\nThe reference implementation mirrored for module SHAPE is\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8122`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8122)\n(Metal Finishing, Plating and Coating Machine Operators -- a closely\nrelated industrial-workshop safety pattern), independently re-cloned\nand read in full this session. Its `platingcoord.{store,advisor,\ngovernor,actor}` module split, `langgraph.graph` StateGraph shape,\nclosed op-allowlist and scope-exclusion self-trip-guard design\ntranslate almost 1:1 onto this occupation's own workshop-hazard\ncoordination scope, so this ADR's `mechassemblycoord.*` modules follow\nthe same shape with domain terms substituted (plater -> assembler,\nplating facility/plant -> assembly line/workshop station,\nplating-operation-execution -> assembly-execution,\nchemical-safety-clearance -> line-safety-clearance). A concurrent\nsibling in this same 82xx-assemblers batch,\n[`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8212`](https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8212)\n(Electrical and Electronic Equipment Assemblers, ADR-2799008212),\nindependently converged on near-identical scope-exclusion phrasing\n(\"finalize the assembly operation\", \"declare the line safety\ncleared\") -- confirming the 82xx subgroup's shared domain vocabulary\nwithout cross-repo coordination.\n\n## Decision\n\nPublish `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8211` as an OSS\ndocumentation/logistics-coordination actor for mechanical machinery\nassemblers, following the SAME governed-actor architecture as every\nprior `cloud-itonami-isco-*` actor in this fleet: **Mechanical\nAssembly Line Scheduling Coordination Advisor ⊣\nMechAssemblyCoordGovernor** on a `langgraph.graph` StateGraph\n(`io.github.kotoba-lang/langgraph`, git-sha-pinned identically to the\nreference's `deps.edn` -- a different library coordinate from the\n`langgraph-clj` used by the unrelated `20-actors/*` actor family),\nwith an independent Governor, human-in-the-loop approval via\n`interrupt-before`, and an append-only audit ledger.\n\nThis actor is deliberately **line scheduling/logistics coordination\nONLY, never assembly-execution authority** -- it must NEVER be\ndesigned to exercise, simulate exercising, or propose exercising an\nassembly-execution decision or a line-safety-clearance decision.\n\n### Closed proposal-op allowlist (all `:effect :propose`)\n\n- `:log-work-record` -- production-run/inventory/progress data\n  logging only\n- `:schedule-crew-operation` -- crew/shift/task scheduling proposal\n  only\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- surface a pinch-point-hazard/tool-hazard/\n  equipment-condition concern, ALWAYS escalates immediately, NEVER\n  auto-commit-eligible\n- `:coordinate-supply-order` -- components/fasteners-stock\n  procurement proposal only\n\nNo op in this allowlist, and no op anywhere in this codebase,\nresembles an assembly-execution finalization decision or a\nline-safety-clearance finalization decision -- these are structurally\nabsent from the op-allowlist entirely, not merely gated behind\nescalation.\n\n### Five HARD governor checks (permanent, un-overridable)\n\n1. **Assembler unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered assembler (crew member) record\n   (`:no-assembler`). Never trusts the proposal's own claim of who the\n   assembler is.\n2. **Line unregistered** -- any proposal must resolve to an\n   independently registered assembly line/workshop station record\n   (`:no-line`).\n3. **Effect not `:propose`** -- any other `:effect` value is a HARD\n   block (`:no-actuation`; the governor never dispatches hardware\n   itself and never performs assembly work itself).\n4. **Op not allowed** -- any `:op` outside the closed four-op\n   allowlist is a HARD, PERMANENT block (`:unknown-op`).\n5. **Scope-excluded action** -- the proposal's `:op` or `:rationale`\n   must never name an assembly-execution finalization, a\n   line-safety-clearance finalization, or a plant-safety-officer-\n   judgment override (`:scope-excluded-action`), REGARDLESS of the\n   declared `:op` or confidence. This is a hard, PERMANENT block --\n   there is no maturity level or confidence at which this actor may\n   auto-commit or human-approve its way past this rule; it is an\n   outright refusal, not an escalation.\n\n### Escalation gates (always human sign-off)\n\n- `:flag-safety-concern` -- ALWAYS escalates to a human, regardless of\n  confidence (governor's always-escalate path). The only channel by\n  which the robot may surface a pinch-point-hazard/tool-hazard/\n  equipment-condition observation -- it never resolves the concern\n  itself.\n- a `:coordinate-supply-order` proposal whose `:cost` exceeds\n  `supply-cost-threshold` (2000) -- always needs human sign-off,\n  independent of confidence (boundary verified as exclusive by\n  `ok-supply-order-at-threshold-boundary`: exactly-at-threshold\n  commits, over-threshold escalates).\n- (advisor confidence below `confidence-floor` 0.6 also escalates, for\n  any op.)\n\n### Self-trip guard (fleet-known bug class, CLAUDE.md)\n\nA governor's scope-exclusion term list phrased as bare NOUNS (e.g.\n\"assembly\", \"fixture\", \"component\") can accidentally match inside the\nmock advisor's own default rationale text for a legitimate proposal,\nself-blocking the actor's own happy path -- and this vertical's domain\nvocabulary is an especially sharp case of that risk, because routine\nwork-log/task text legitimately names assembly components and\nfixtures by name (e.g. a task literally described as \"routine\nmechanical assembly task with pinch-point hazard and crush hazard\nfixtures and fastener components\" -- component identification, not a\nproposal to finalize an assembly operation or clear a line as safe).\n`mechassemblycoord.governor/scope-excluded-phrases` is phrased\nexclusively as finalization ACTION PHRASES (\"proceed with the\nassembly run\", \"finalize the assembly operation\", \"declare the line\nsafety cleared\", \"override the plant safety officer's judgment\"),\nnever bare nouns, and is checked only against the proposal's\n`:rationale` field (the advisor's own generated text), never against\nraw `:task` input text.\n`mechassemblycoord.governor-test/default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-\ntrip-on-scope-exclusion` is a dedicated regression test driving the\ndefault mock-advisor across all four allowed ops with a task field\ncontaining exactly those bare component/hazard nouns, asserting none\nof them ever hard-trip the finalization-scope rule. Green on first\nrun.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry's `{:id \"8211\" ...}` entry\n  promoted from `:spec` to `:implemented`, with `:repo`/`:business-id`\n  set to the real repository\n  (`https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8211` /\n  `cloud-itonami-isco-8211`), and `maturity-summary`'s implemented\n  count bumped accordingly (recomputed live via\n  `kotoba.occupation/maturity-summary` immediately before the edit,\n  not assumed, to account for concurrent sibling landings in this same\n  6-agent batch: 112 spec / 0 blueprint / 324 implemented at that\n  fetch).\n- Full test suite (`clojure -M:test`): 32 tests, 69 assertions, 0\n  failures, 0 errors -- raw output captured in this ADR's `:test/\n  counts` and independently re-verified against a brand-new fresh\n  clone of the pushed repository (not just the local working copy). No\n  mojibake in the Japanese violation-detail strings (0\n  replacement-byte instances grepped in the fresh clone).\n- No `manifest/west.yml` changes -- this actor is registered purely\n  via the `kotoba-lang/occupation` capability registry, consistent\n  with sibling `cloud-itonami-isco-*`/`cloud-itonami-isic-*` actors.\n- No `kotoba-lang/technology` dependency was added to the actor's\n  `deps.edn` -- only the central `kotoba-lang/occupation` registry\n  itself depends on it, to resolve `:required-technologies` stacks.\n- This ADR is EDN-only, matching the live `90-docs/adr` convention\n  (commit `6d2d0eeda658`) -- a single-entity EDN vector with an\n  `:adr/body` string blob rather than a paired `.md`+`.edn`.\n\n## Alternatives considered\n\n- Giving the actor an op like `:approve-assembly-run`/`:approve-\n  line-safety-clearance` that is merely gated behind an\n  always-escalate path: rejected outright per the task's explicit\n  guidance -- an always-escalate op still means the *capability* to\n  finalize an assembly operation or a line-safety clearance exists in\n  the code path and is merely deferred to a human gate. For a decision\n  whose failure mode is hand-tool injury or a pinch-point/crush\n  incident, the capability itself must not exist in this actor at\n  all.\n- A bare-noun scope-exclusion term list (e.g. `[\"assembly\" \"fixture\"\n  \"component\"]`): rejected outright per the fleet-known self-trip bug\n  class described above and the task's explicit CRITICAL warning --\n  this domain's own vocabulary necessarily discusses assembly\n  components and fixtures by name to do its logging/scheduling job, so\n  a bare-noun list would self-block the actor's own legitimate\n  proposals whenever a task description mentions those parts.\n- Naming the crew-member field `:operator-id` (matching a literal\n  reading of \"assembler\"'s deploying-business sense): rejected --\n  `docs/operator-guide.md` and this fleet's convention already use\n  \"operator\" to mean the deploying business/entity running the actor,\n  so reusing the same word for the individual crew member would create\n  a confusing collision; `:assembler-id` (mirroring the reference's\n  `:plater-id` pattern) keeps the two senses distinct.\n\n## Test verification\n\nFresh clone re-run immediately before writing this ADR:\n\n```text\nRunning tests in #{\"test\"}\n\nTesting mechassemblycoord.actor-test\n\nTesting mechassemblycoord.governor-test\n\nRan 32 tests containing 69 assertions.\n0 failures, 0 errors.\n```\n", :adr/date "2026-07-18", :test/counts "{:total 32, :assertions 69, :failures 0, :errors 0}", :actor/coordination? true, :db/id -1, :test/coverage [:governor-hard-checks :advisor-shapes :integration :store-contract :scope-exclusion-self-trip-regression :assembler-verification :line-verification :closed-op-allowlist :supply-cost-threshold-escalation :assembly-execution-line-safety-clearance-finalization-forbidden :plant-safety-officer-override-forbidden], :registry/business-id "cloud-itonami-isco-8211", :registry/repo "https://github.com/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8211", :adr/scope [:kotoba-lang/occupation :cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isco-8211], :test/simulator :mechassemblycoord.actor-test, :adr/title "cloud-itonami-isco-8211 — Mechanical Machinery Assemblers Assembly-Line Scheduling/Logistics Coordination Coverage", :adr/source-format "edn", :adr/references [2607121000], :actor/id :cloud-itonami-isco-8211, :actor/name "Mechanical Machinery Assembly Line Scheduling and Logistics Coordination Practice"}]
